Algorithm:

Step 1: Arrange all intervals in increasing order based on their ending values.
Step 2: Select the first interval as the previously chosen interval.
Step 3: Initialize a counter to keep track of the number of overlapping intervals to remove.
Step 4: Traverse the remaining intervals one by one.
Step 5: For each interval, compare its starting value with the ending value of the previously selected interval.
Step 6: If the starting value is less than the previous ending value, an overlap occurs, so increase the removal counter.
Step 7: If there is no overlap, update the previously selected interval to the current interval.
Step 8: Continue this process until all intervals are checked.
Step 9: Return the total number of intervals that must be removed.

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: O(1)


-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    public int eraseOverlapIntervals(int[][] intervals) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(a->a[1]));
        int ans=0;
        int []prev=intervals[0];
        for(int i=1;i<intervals.length;i++){
            if(prev[1]>intervals[i][0]){
                ans++;
            }else{
                prev=intervals[i];
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
